{0 wgpu — OCaml bindings to WebGPU through wgpu-native}

OCaml bindings to the WebGPU C API ([webgpu.h]) and the wgpu-native
extensions ([wgpu.h]), generated from the vendored upstream headers of one
pinned {{:https://github.com/gfx-rs/wgpu-native}wgpu-native} release and bound
with {{:https://github.com/yallop/ocaml-ctypes}ctypes-foreign}.
[libwgpu_native] is opened with [dlopen] on first use: no C stubs are
compiled, no headers are needed at build time, and there is nothing to link.

{b This project was written entirely by AI.} The generator, the runtime, the
generated bindings, the tests, the examples, the CI workflows and this page
were produced by AI agents with no hand-written human code; the only files
that are not AI-authored are the vendored upstream headers under [vendor/].
Correctness rests on automated checks — a C ABI probe compiled from the
vendored headers, an independent header rescan, and real headless GPU runs —
not on human review. See
{{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/#authorship}Authorship} for the
caveats, and {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/#licensing}Licensing}:
no licence has been chosen for the original code yet.

{1 Where to start}

- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/guide.html}The guide} — an
  example-driven walkthrough: installing the native library, the two layers,
  handles, enums and bit sets, struct defaults, descriptors and lifetimes,
  compute, offscreen rendering, callbacks and polling, errors, and what to do
  when the ergonomic layer stops.
- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/}Project home} — install,
  quick start, status, limitations.
- {{:https://lloyd-g-w.github.io/wgpu-ocaml/design.html}Design contract} —
  the rules the generator, the runtime and the tests must keep.
- {!Wgpu} — the API reference below.

{1 Layout of the [Wgpu] module}

The library has two layers and they share the same types, so they mix freely.

- {b The raw layer}, generated from the headers:
  {!Wgpu.Types} (enums, bit sets, opaque handles, structs, callback types) and
  {!Wgpu.Fn} (one OCaml function per C entry point, under its exact C name,
  e.g. {!Wgpu.Fn.wgpuDeviceCreateBuffer}). Struct fields keep their C
  spelling, so the headers stay greppable from OCaml, and every struct module
  has an [init ()] that applies the header's [WGPU_*_INIT] defaults.
- {b The ergonomic layer}, hand written: {!Wgpu.Instance}, {!Wgpu.Adapter},
  {!Wgpu.Device}, {!Wgpu.Queue}, {!Wgpu.Buffer}, {!Wgpu.Command_encoder},
  {!Wgpu.Compute_pass}, {!Wgpu.Render_pass}, {!Wgpu.Texture},
  {!Wgpu.Shader_module}, {!Wgpu.Compute_pipeline}, {!Wgpu.Render_pipeline},
  {!Wgpu.Bind_group}, {!Wgpu.Command_buffer} and {!Wgpu.Log}. Lifetimes are
  explicit — every [create]/[request] has a matching [release] — and WebGPU
  errors are raised as {!Wgpu.Error}.
- {b Runtime plumbing}: {!Wgpu.Loader} (search path, [dlopen], version gate),
  {!Wgpu.Pin} (the pinned upstream release and its checksums),
  {!Wgpu.Callback} (keeping OCaml closures alive across C calls) and
  {!Wgpu.Abi} (the layout dump used by the ABI test).

{1 A taste}

{[
open Wgpu

let () =
  let instance = Instance.create () in
  let adapter = Instance.request_adapter instance in
  let device = Adapter.request_device ~label:"demo" adapter in
  let queue = Device.queue device in
  let buffer =
    Device.create_buffer device ~label:"data" ~size:1024
      (* copy_dst is what makes Queue.write_buffer legal *)
      ~usage:Types.BufferUsage.(combine [ storage; copy_dst; copy_src ])
  in
  Queue.write_buffer queue buffer (Bytes.make 1024 '\000');
  Buffer.release buffer;
  Queue.release queue;
  Device.release device;
  Adapter.release adapter;
  Instance.release instance
]}

Anything the ergonomic layer does not cover — surfaces and swapchains, render
bundles, query sets, samplers, vertex buffer layouts, depth/stencil and blend
state, explicit pipeline layouts, SPIR-V shaders — is one {!Wgpu.Fn} call away
on the very same handles.

{1 Things worth knowing before you start}

- {b Start every struct from [init ()]}, never from [Ctypes.make]: zero is not
  a valid default in WebGPU (undefined limits are [0xFFFFFFFF], a depth clear
  value is [NaN], an extension struct's [sType] must be set).
- {b Lifetimes are yours.} No finaliser releases a GPU handle for you.
- {b One domain at a time.} Callbacks may arrive on threads wgpu-native owns,
  so entry points release the OCaml domain lock and closures register the
  calling thread and re-take it; what is not supported is driving wgpu from
  several {e domains}, since registered foreign threads attach to domain 0.
- {b Futures do not work in this release.} [WGPUFuture] and
  [wgpuInstanceWaitAny] are not implemented in wgpu-native v29; asynchronous
  work is driven by {!Wgpu.Device.poll} and
  {!Wgpu.Instance.process_events} instead.
- {b Some bound entry points abort the process} because wgpu-native leaves
  them [unimplemented!()]. They are listed in {!Wgpu.Pin.unimplemented}.
